\begin{abstract}
We give a complete quantitative theory of the displacement induced by the
Kullback--Leibler-regularized preference-optimization objective
$J(q) = \E_q[r] - \beta\,\KL(q\|p) + \gamma\,\E_d[\log q]$ on a finite response space,
where $p$ is the reference (supervised fine-tuned) policy, $r$ a reward model, $d$ a
pretraining distribution, and $\beta,\gamma>0$ regularization coefficients. The optimizer
of the $\gamma=0$ objective is the exponential tilt $\pi_\beta \propto p\,e^{r/\beta}$,
and we quantify precisely how far $\pi_\beta$ can be from $p$, in what currency, and which
functionals of the policy are able to move. We prove: a Pinsker inequality
$\|q-p\|_1^2 \le 2\KL(q\|p)$ from a Pad\'e-type bound for the logarithm, with the constant
$2$ shown optimal; the self-limiting bound $\KL(\pi_\beta\|p) \le \range(r)/\beta$; its
strict improvement to $\KL(\pi_\beta\|p) \le \frac{\range(r)^2}{2\beta^2}e^{\range(r)/\beta}$
with a matching lower bound establishing drift $\Theta(\beta^{-1})$; the replacement of the
range by the reference standard deviation, $\|\pi_\beta-p\|_1 \le \beta^{-1}\sqrt{2e^{\range(r)/\beta}\Var_p(r)}$,
with a closed-form two-point computation $\|\pi_\beta-p\|_1 = \tanh(a/2\beta)$ proving the
law $\Theta(\sigma_p(r)/\beta)$; a first-order expansion of the audit gap,
$|\E_{\pi_\beta}[f]-\E_p[f]-\Cov_p(r,f)/\beta| \le 24(R/\beta)^2\sigma_p(f)$ for $|r|\le R\le\beta$,
whence statistics uncorrelated with the reward move only at order $\beta^{-2}$; two-sided
gain--drift inequalities and a budget inequality
$\beta\KL(q\|p)+\gamma\KL(d\|q) \le \range(r)+\gamma\KL(d\|p)$ for the pretraining mix-in;
and a zero-temperature analysis showing total policy collapse as $\beta \downarrow 0$.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}

\subsection{The objective}

Preference optimization of a generative model is standardly posed as the maximization of
\begin{equation}
J(q) \;=\; \underbrace{\E_{y\sim q}[r(y)]}_{\text{reward}} \;-\; \underbrace{\beta\,\KL(q\|p)}_{\text{anchor}} \;+\; \underbrace{\gamma\,\E_{y\sim d}[\log q(y)]}_{\text{pretraining mix-in}}
\label{eq:obj}
\end{equation}
over policies $q$, where $p$ is a reference model, $r$ is a learned or rule-based reward,
$d$ is the pretraining distribution, and $\beta,\gamma>0$. The three terms encode three
competing desiderata: score highly, do not move, do not forget. Nothing below depends on
how $r$ is produced: it may be a neural preference model, a symbolic verifier, or a mixture.

The qualitative solution of \eqref{eq:obj} with $\gamma=0$ is classical: the maximizer is the
Gibbs tilt of the reference. The quantitative content --- how far the optimizer travels, and
which observables it disturbs --- is the subject of this paper.

\subsection{What is folklore and what is proved}

The alignment literature routinely asserts that the anchor term ``prevents policy collapse
and reward hacking''. Formalizing that assertion requires three separate answers: how far
the policy moves (in which metric, at what rate, with which functional of $r$ as constant);
which functionals of the policy move (a selective, not uniform, statement); and what the
anchor costs in achievable reward. We answer all three, with matching examples establishing
sharpness where possible.

\subsection{Setting and notation}

Throughout, $\Om$ is a finite nonempty set of responses. A \emph{distribution} is
$p:\Om\to\mathbb{R}$ with $p\ge 0$ and $\sum_y p(y)=1$; it is \emph{positive} if $p(y)>0$ for
all $y$. We write $\E_p[f]=\sum_y p(y)f(y)$, $\Var_p(f)=\E_p[(f-\E_p f)^2]$,
$\sigma_p(f)=\sqrt{\Var_p(f)}$, $\Cov_p(f,g)=\E_p[(f-\E_p f)(g-\E_p g)]$,
$\KL(q\|p)=\sum_y q(y)\log\frac{q(y)}{p(y)}$ and $\|q-p\|_1=\sum_y|q(y)-p(y)|$. The
\emph{reward range} is $\range(r)=\max_y r(y)-\min_y r(y)$.

\begin{definition}[Aligned policy]
For $\beta>0$, a reward $r$ and a positive reference $p$, set
\[
Z_\beta=\sum_y p(y)e^{r(y)/\beta},\qquad F_\beta=\beta\log Z_\beta,\qquad
T_\beta(y)=\frac{e^{r(y)/\beta}}{Z_\beta},\qquad \pi_\beta(y)=p(y)T_\beta(y).
\]
Thus $\pi_\beta$ is a positive distribution, $\pi_\beta = p\cdot T_\beta$ pointwise, and
$\E_p[T_\beta]=1$. That $\pi_\beta$ maximizes \eqref{eq:obj} with $\gamma=0$, with optimal
value $F_\beta$, is the Gibbs variational principle.
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}[Divergence of the tilt]\label{lem:kltilt}
$\KL(\pi_\beta\|p) = \E_{\pi_\beta}\!\left[r/\beta\right] - \log Z_\beta$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Pointwise $\pi_\beta(y)/p(y)=e^{r(y)/\beta}/Z_\beta$, so
$\log(\pi_\beta(y)/p(y)) = r(y)/\beta - \log Z_\beta$; sum against $\pi_\beta$ and use
$\sum_y\pi_\beta(y)=1$.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Shift invariance]\label{lem:shift}
$\pi_\beta$ is unchanged when $r$ is replaced by $r+c$ for a constant $c$. Hence every bound
below may be proved for the centered reward $r-\tfrac12(\max r+\min r)$, which satisfies
$\|r\|_\infty \le \range(r)/2$.
\end{lemma}

\section{Pinsker's inequality and its sharpness}

\begin{lemma}[Pad\'e-type logarithm bound]\label{lem:pade}
For all $t>0$, $\;\log t \ge \dfrac{5t^2-4t-1}{2t^2+4t}$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Let $\Phi(t)=\log t-\frac{5t^2-4t-1}{2t^2+4t}$. A direct computation gives
$\Phi'(t)=\frac{(t-1)^3}{t^2(t+2)^2}$, negative on $(0,1)$ and positive on $(1,\infty)$; so
$\Phi$ attains its global minimum at $t=1$, where $\Phi(1)=0$. The right-hand side is a
$(2,2)$ Pad\'e-type approximant of $\log$ at $t=1$: the defect vanishes to third order,
which is what makes the resulting Pinsker constant sharp.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Pointwise divergence slack]\label{lem:slack}
For $a\ge 0$ and $b>0$, $\;a\log\frac{a}{b}-a+b \ge \dfrac{3(a-b)^2}{2(a+2b)}$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{theorem}[Pinsker]\label{thm:pinsker}
For a distribution $q$ and a positive distribution $p$ on $\Om$,
$\;\|q-p\|_1^2 \le 2\,\KL(q\|p)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Since $\sum_y q(y)=\sum_y p(y)=1$ we may write
$\KL(q\|p)=\sum_y\big(q(y)\log\frac{q(y)}{p(y)}-q(y)+p(y)\big)$, a sum of nonnegative terms.
Bounding each by Lemma~\ref{lem:slack} and applying Cauchy--Schwarz in Engel form,
\[
\sum_y \frac{(q(y)-p(y))^2}{q(y)+2p(y)} \;\ge\; \frac{\big(\sum_y|q(y)-p(y)|\big)^2}{\sum_y(q(y)+2p(y))}=\frac{\|q-p\|_1^2}{3},
\]
gives $\KL(q\|p)\ge \tfrac32\cdot\tfrac13\|q-p\|_1^2=\tfrac12\|q-p\|_1^2$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Optimality of the constant $2$]\label{thm:pinskersharp}
Let $q_\varepsilon=(\tfrac12+\varepsilon,\tfrac12-\varepsilon)$ and $q_0$ the uniform
distribution on a two-element space. Then
$2\KL(q_\varepsilon\|q_0)/\|q_\varepsilon-q_0\|_1^2 \to 1$ as $\varepsilon\downarrow0$;
consequently for every $c<2$ the inequality $\|q-p\|_1^2\le c\,\KL(q\|p)$ fails.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Two cubic bounds, each proved by the mean-value technique of Lemma~\ref{lem:pade} (the defect
functions have derivatives $x^3/(1\pm x)$):
$\log(1+x)\le x-\tfrac{x^2}{2}+\tfrac{x^3}{3}$ for $x\ge0$, and
$\log(1-x)\le -x-\tfrac{x^2}{2}-\tfrac{x^3}{3}$ for $0\le x<1$. With $x=2\varepsilon$ they give
$2\KL(q_\varepsilon\|q_0)\le\|q_\varepsilon-q_0\|_1^2(1+\tfrac83\varepsilon^2)$, while
Theorem~\ref{thm:pinsker} gives the reverse with factor $1$.
\end{proof}

Because the Pinsker step is optimal, any improvement of a drift law obtained by composing
``divergence bound $\to$ Pinsker'' must come from improving the divergence bound.

\section{First drift law: the anchor is self-limiting}

\begin{theorem}[Self-limiting divergence]\label{thm:selflimit}
For every $\beta>0$, $\;\KL(\pi_\beta\|p) \le \range(r)/\beta$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
By Lemma~\ref{lem:kltilt}, $\beta\KL(\pi_\beta\|p)=\E_{\pi_\beta}[r]-F_\beta$. Jensen gives
$F_\beta=\beta\log\E_p[e^{r/\beta}]\ge\E_p[r]$, so
$\beta\KL(\pi_\beta\|p)\le\E_{\pi_\beta}[r]-\E_p[r]\le\max r-\min r$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Square-root no-collapse law]
$\|\pi_\beta-p\|_1\le\sqrt{2\range(r)/\beta}\to0$ as $\beta\to\infty$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{corollary}[Uniform audit bound]\label{cor:uniformaudit}
If $|f|\le M$ pointwise then
$|\E_{\pi_\beta}[f]-\E_p[f]| \le M\|\pi_\beta-p\|_1 \le M\sqrt{2\range(r)/\beta}$.
\end{corollary}

\section{The exact rate is $\beta^{-1}$}

\begin{lemma}[Elementary exponential estimates]\label{lem:exp}
For all real $x$: $1+x\le e^x$ and $|e^x-1|\le|x|e^{|x|}$; and $|e^x-1-x|\le x^2$ for $|x|\le1$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{theorem}[Quadratic divergence bound]\label{thm:quad}
For $\beta>0$, $\;\KL(\pi_\beta\|p) \le \dfrac{\range(r)^2}{2\beta^2}\,e^{\range(r)/\beta}$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
By Lemma~\ref{lem:shift} assume $|r|\le m:=\range(r)/2$. By Lemma~\ref{lem:kltilt} and Jensen,
$\KL(\pi_\beta\|p)\le \frac1\beta\big(\E_{\pi_\beta}[r]-\E_p[r]\big)$. By
Lemma~\ref{lem:reweight} below this equals $\frac1\beta\Cov_p(T_\beta,r)$, and the tilt obeys
$|T_\beta(x)-T_\beta(y)|\le\frac{|r(x)-r(y)|}{\beta}e^{\range(r)/\beta}$, obtained from
$|e^a-e^b|\le|a-b|\max(e^a,e^b)$ and $Z_\beta\ge e^{\min r/\beta}$. Feeding this into the
covariance yields the factor $\range(r)^2/(2\beta^2)$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Linear no-collapse law]\label{cor:linear}
$\|\pi_\beta-p\|_1 \le \frac{\range(r)}{\beta}e^{\range(r)/(2\beta)}$, and
$\|\pi_\beta-p\|_1 \le 2\range(r)/\beta$ whenever $\beta\ge\range(r)$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{theorem}[The rate is attained]\label{thm:lower}
Let $\Om=\{\mathtt{t},\mathtt{f}\}$, $p$ uniform, $r=\mathbf 1_{\{\mathtt t\}}$, so $\range(r)=1$.
Then $\|\pi_\beta-p\|_1 \ge \frac{1}{3\beta}$ for every $\beta\ge2$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Here $Z_\beta=(e^{1/\beta}+1)/2$ and $\|\pi_\beta-p\|_1=\frac{e^{1/\beta}-1}{e^{1/\beta}+1}$
exactly; use $e^x\ge1+x$ in the numerator and $e^{1/\beta}\le e^{1/2}\le2$ in the denominator.
\end{proof}

Combining Corollary~\ref{cor:linear} and Theorem~\ref{thm:lower}, alignment drift is
$\Theta(\beta^{-1})$: the $\beta^{-1/2}$ law is not tight, even though the Pinsker step inside
its proof is.

\section{The correct constant is the reward standard deviation}

\begin{lemma}[Pair representation]\label{lem:pair}
For a distribution $p$ and any $f$,
$\Var_p(f)=\tfrac12\sum_x\sum_y p(x)p(y)(f(x)-f(y))^2$. Consequently, if
$|f(x)-f(y)|\le c$ for all $x,y$ with $c \ge 0$ then $\sigma_p(f)\le c$; and if
$|f(x)-f(y)|\le c|g(x)-g(y)|$ for all $x,y$ then $\Var_p(f)\le c^2\Var_p(g)$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{lemma}[Reweighting identity]\label{lem:reweight}
For every statistic $f$, $\;\E_{\pi_\beta}[f]-\E_p[f]=\Cov_p(T_\beta,f)$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
$\E_{\pi_\beta}[f]=\E_p[T_\beta f]$ and $\E_p[T_\beta]=1$, so
$\E_p[T_\beta f]-\E_p[f]=\E_p[T_\beta f]-\E_p[T_\beta]\E_p[f]=\Cov_p(T_\beta,f)$.
\end{proof}

This identity is the conceptual centre of the paper: every change produced by alignment is a
reference covariance with the likelihood ratio $T_\beta$.

\begin{theorem}[Variance of the tilt]\label{thm:vartilt}
$\Var_p(T_\beta) \le \left(\dfrac{e^{\range(r)/\beta}}{\beta}\right)^{\!2}\Var_p(r)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Variance drift law]\label{thm:vardrift}
For $\beta>0$,
\[
\KL(\pi_\beta\|p) \le e^{\range(r)/\beta}\frac{\Var_p(r)}{\beta^2},
\qquad
\|\pi_\beta-p\|_1 \le \frac{\sqrt{2e^{\range(r)/\beta}\Var_p(r)}}{\beta}.
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
By Lemma~\ref{lem:kltilt} and Jensen, $\KL(\pi_\beta\|p)\le\frac1\beta\Cov_p(T_\beta,r)$ using
Lemma~\ref{lem:reweight}; Cauchy--Schwarz gives $|\Cov_p(T_\beta,r)|\le\sigma_p(T_\beta)\sigma_p(r)$
and Theorem~\ref{thm:vartilt} bounds $\sigma_p(T_\beta)$. The metric form is
Theorem~\ref{thm:pinsker}.
\end{proof}

\begin{remark}[Never weaker, sometimes much stronger]\label{rem:popoviciu}
Popoviciu's inequality $\Var_p(r)\le\range(r)^2/4$ shows Theorem~\ref{thm:vardrift} always
improves on Theorem~\ref{thm:quad} by at least a factor $2$. The improvement is unbounded:
let $p$ give mass $1-\delta$ to a set on which $r\equiv0$ and mass $\delta$ to one response
with $r=R$; then $\range(r)=R$ while $\Var_p(r)=\delta(1-\delta)R^2\to0$. This is the profile
of a reward model with a rare exploitable hole; the theorem says such a hole cannot be found
by tilting alone at high $\beta$.
\end{remark}

\begin{corollary}[Zero contrast, zero drift]
If $\Var_p(r)=0$ then $\pi_\beta=p$ exactly, for every $\beta>0$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{theorem}[Sharpness of the standard-deviation law]\label{thm:varsharp}
Let $\Om=\{\mathtt t,\mathtt f\}$, $p$ uniform, and $r_a=a\mathbf 1_{\{\mathtt t\}}$ with $a>0$.
Then, exactly,
\[
\|\pi_\beta-p\|_1=\frac{e^{a/\beta}-1}{e^{a/\beta}+1}=\tanh\!\Big(\frac{a}{2\beta}\Big),
\qquad \Var_p(r_a)=\frac{a^2}{4},\quad \sigma:=\sigma_p(r_a)=\frac a2,
\]
and for $0<a\le\beta$, $\;\dfrac{\sigma}{2\beta}\le\|\pi_\beta-p\|_1\le\dfrac{3\sigma}{\beta}$.
\end{theorem}

Hence the drift law is exactly $\Theta(\sigma_p(r)/\beta)$; only the absolute constant remains
open. The cumulant heuristic --- $\beta\log Z_\beta$ is the cumulant generating function of $r$
evaluated at $1/\beta$, whose curvature at $0$ is $\Var_p(r)$ --- predicts
$\KL(\pi_\beta\|p)=\Var_p(r)/(2\beta^2)+O(\beta^{-3})$.

\section{Which statistics move: the audit gap is a covariance}

Let $f:\Om\to\mathbb R$ be an \emph{audit statistic}: an unseen reward model, a safety
classifier, a refusal-rate indicator. The \emph{audit gap} is $\E_{\pi_\beta}[f]-\E_p[f]$.

\begin{theorem}[Fluctuation, not magnitude]\label{thm:auditstd}
$\big|\E_{\pi_\beta}[f]-\E_p[f]\big| \le \dfrac{e^{\range(r)/\beta}}{\beta}\sigma_p(r)\sigma_p(f)$.
\end{theorem}

This is strictly better than Corollary~\ref{cor:uniformaudit} in two ways: the rate is
$\beta^{-1}$, and the size of $f$ is measured by its reference standard deviation rather than
its supremum. A statistic nearly deterministic under $p$ cannot be moved at any temperature.

\begin{lemma}[Bilinearity]\label{lem:bilin}
$\Cov_p(g-h,f)=\Cov_p(g,f)-\Cov_p(h,f)$ and $\Cov_p(g/c,f)=\Cov_p(g,f)/c$ for $c\ne0$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{lemma}[Partition function near $1$]\label{lem:Znear1}
If $|r|\le R\le\beta$ then $\tfrac13\le Z_\beta$ and $|Z_\beta-1|\le3R/\beta$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
$Z_\beta\ge e^{\min r/\beta}\ge e^{-R/\beta}\ge e^{-1}\ge\tfrac13$ using $R\le\beta$ and $e\le3$.
For the second, $Z_\beta-1=\E_p[e^{r/\beta}-1]$ and $|e^x-1|\le|x|e^{|x|}\le 3R/\beta$ pointwise.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[The tilt is its own linearization]\label{lem:lin}
If $|r|\le R\le\beta$ then for all $x,y\in\Om$,
\[
\Big|\big(T_\beta(x)-\tfrac{r(x)}{\beta}\big)-\big(T_\beta(y)-\tfrac{r(y)}{\beta}\big)\Big| \;\le\; 24\Big(\frac R\beta\Big)^{2}.
\]
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Write $u=r/\beta$, so $|u|\le R/\beta\le1$, and decompose
\[
T_\beta(y)-u(y)=\frac{e^{u(y)}-1-u(y)}{Z_\beta}+\frac{1}{Z_\beta}+u(y)\Big(\frac1{Z_\beta}-1\Big).
\]
The middle term is constant in $y$ and contributes no oscillation. The first has magnitude at
most $3(R/\beta)^2$ by Lemma~\ref{lem:exp} and $1/Z_\beta\le3$, hence oscillation $6(R/\beta)^2$.
The third has magnitude at most $9(R/\beta)^2$ since $|1/Z_\beta-1|\le 9R/\beta$ by
Lemma~\ref{lem:Znear1}, hence oscillation $18(R/\beta)^2$. Sum: $24(R/\beta)^2$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[First-order expansion of the audit gap]\label{thm:firstorder}
Let $|r|\le R\le\beta$. Then for every statistic $f$,
\[
\left|\;\E_{\pi_\beta}[f]-\E_p[f]-\frac{\Cov_p(r,f)}{\beta}\;\right| \;\le\; 24\left(\frac R\beta\right)^{2}\sigma_p(f).
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
By Lemma~\ref{lem:reweight} the gap is $\Cov_p(T_\beta,f)$. Split
$T_\beta=\frac r\beta+(T_\beta-\frac r\beta)$ and use Lemma~\ref{lem:bilin}:
$\Cov_p(T_\beta,f)=\Cov_p(r,f)/\beta+\Cov_p(T_\beta-r/\beta,f)$. Cauchy--Schwarz bounds the
remainder by $\sigma_p(T_\beta-r/\beta)\sigma_p(f)$, and Lemmas~\ref{lem:pair}
and~\ref{lem:lin} give $\sigma_p(T_\beta-r/\beta)\le24(R/\beta)^2$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Uncorrelated statistics are second-order safe]\label{cor:uncorr}
If $\Cov_p(r,f)=0$ and $|r|\le R\le\beta$, then
$|\E_{\pi_\beta}[f]-\E_p[f]| \le 24(R/\beta)^2\sigma_p(f)$.
\end{corollary}

The hypothesis is checkable before alignment: $\Cov_p(r,f)$ is an expectation under the
reference policy, estimable by sampling from the model one already has. The conclusion is an
order-of-magnitude improvement: the generic guarantee is $O(\beta^{-1})$, while uncorrelated
statistics enjoy $O(\beta^{-2})$. This is the precise sense in which first-order reward
hacking requires correlation with the reward model.

\section{The price of alignment}

Let $G_\beta=\E_{\pi_\beta}[r]-\E_p[r]$ denote the reward gain.

\begin{theorem}[Gain costs drift]\label{thm:gaincost}
$G_\beta \ge \beta\KL(\pi_\beta\|p) \ge \frac\beta2\|\pi_\beta-p\|_1^2$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Optimality gives $\E_{\pi_\beta}[r]-\beta\KL(\pi_\beta\|p)=F_\beta$ and Jensen gives
$F_\beta\ge\E_p[r]$; subtract, then apply Theorem~\ref{thm:pinsker}.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Drift caps gain]\label{thm:driftcap}
$G_\beta \le \frac{\range(r)}{2}\|\pi_\beta-p\|_1$, and $G_\beta\le\range(r)^2/\beta$ if
$\beta\ge\range(r)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Centering $r$ at $c=\tfrac12(\max r+\min r)$ leaves $G_\beta$ unchanged and makes
$|r-c|\le\range(r)/2$; then $G_\beta=\sum_y(\pi_\beta(y)-p(y))(r(y)-c)$. Substitute
Corollary~\ref{cor:linear} for the second claim.
\end{proof}

Now restore $\gamma>0$, writing $J_\gamma$ for the full objective \eqref{eq:obj}. No
optimality is needed: any policy that merely beats the reference obeys a budget.

\begin{theorem}[PTX budget]\label{thm:ptx}
Let $p,q$ be positive distributions, $d$ a distribution, and suppose $J_\gamma(p)\le J_\gamma(q)$.
Then $\;\beta\KL(q\|p)+\gamma\KL(d\|q) \le \range(r)+\gamma\KL(d\|p)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Expand both sides using $\KL(p\|p)=0$ and rewrite each cross-entropy as
$\E_d[\log u]=-H(d)-\KL(d\|u)$; the entropies cancel, leaving
$\beta\KL(q\|p)+\gamma\KL(d\|q)\le(\E_q[r]-\E_p[r])+\gamma\KL(d\|p)$, and
$\E_q[r]-\E_p[r]\le\max r-\min r$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Two independent budgets]
Under the hypotheses of Theorem~\ref{thm:ptx}, with $\beta,\gamma>0$:
\[
\KL(q\|p)\le\frac{\range(r)+\gamma\KL(d\|p)}{\beta},\qquad
\|q-p\|_1\le\sqrt{\frac{2(\range(r)+\gamma\KL(d\|p))}{\beta}},\qquad
\KL(d\|q)\le\KL(d\|p)+\frac{\range(r)}{\gamma}.
\]
\end{corollary}

The reward term can purchase at most $\range(r)/\beta$ of divergence from the fine-tuned
reference, enlarged by exactly $\gamma\KL(d\|p)/\beta$, the reference's own pretraining
mismatch. Symmetrically the reward can push the policy at most $\range(r)/\gamma$ in
divergence away from the pretraining distribution beyond where the reference already sat:
a formal no-catastrophic-forgetting guarantee, with $\gamma$ the explicit dial.

\section{The low-temperature phase: collapse}

Let $r^\star=\max_y r(y)$ and $p_{\min}=\min_y p(y)>0$.

\begin{theorem}[Two-sided Laplace estimate]\label{thm:laplace}
For all $\beta>0$, $\;r^\star+\beta\log p_{\min} \le \beta\log Z_\beta \le r^\star$; hence
$\beta\log Z_\beta\to r^\star$ as $\beta\downarrow0$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
$Z_\beta\le\sum_y p(y)e^{r^\star/\beta}=e^{r^\star/\beta}$; and keeping only a maximizing term,
$Z_\beta\ge p_{\min}e^{r^\star/\beta}$. This is a non-asymptotic, finite-space form of
Varadhan's lemma.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Exponential suppression]\label{thm:suppress}
For all $y$ and $\beta>0$, $\;\pi_\beta(y)\le p_{\min}^{-1}e^{-(r^\star-r(y))/\beta}$; hence
$\pi_\beta(y)\to0$ as $\beta\downarrow0$ whenever $r(y)<r^\star$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Total collapse]\label{thm:collapse}
In the two-point model of Theorem~\ref{thm:lower}, $\|\pi_\beta-p\|_1\to1$ as
$\beta\downarrow0$ --- the maximal value for that model: the aligned policy converges to a
point mass on the reward maximizer.
\end{theorem}

The complete phase picture of the anchor is therefore: total collapse at $\beta=0^+$; drift
$\Theta(\sigma_p(r)/\beta)$ at large $\beta$; exact recovery of the reference at $\beta=\infty$.
Low temperature is not a continuous deformation of the reference, so no perturbative statement
survives there.

\section{Algorithms and estimation}

\paragraph{Algorithm A (exact tilt and drift diagnostics).}
Given $p$, $r$, $\beta$: compute $u_y=r(y)/\beta$, subtract $\max_y u_y$ for stability, form
$w_y=p(y)e^{u_y-\max u}$, normalize to obtain $\pi_\beta$, and return
$\KL(\pi_\beta\|p)=\sum_y\pi_\beta(y)(u_y-\log Z_\beta)$ and $\|\pi_\beta-p\|_1$. Cost
$O(|\Om|)$.

\paragraph{Algorithm B (pre-alignment audit ranking).}
Given samples $y_1,\dots,y_N\sim p$, reward values $r(y_i)$ and audit statistics
$f^{(1)},\dots,f^{(K)}$, compute empirical covariances $\widehat\Cov(r,f^{(k)})$ and standard
deviations $\hat\sigma(f^{(k)})$; report the predicted first-order gap
$\widehat\Cov(r,f^{(k)})/\beta$ with error bar $24(R/\beta)^2\hat\sigma(f^{(k)})$ and rank.
Cost $O(NK)$, with no alignment run required. By Theorem~\ref{thm:firstorder} the ranking is
correct up to the stated $O(\beta^{-2})$ error; by Corollary~\ref{cor:uncorr} statistics with
vanishing empirical covariance are certified second-order safe.

\paragraph{Algorithm C (temperature selection from a drift specification).}
Given a drift tolerance $\varepsilon$ and estimates of $\sigma_p(r)$ and $\range(r)$, binary
search for the least $\beta$ with $\beta^{-1}\sqrt{2e^{\range(r)/\beta}\Var_p(r)}\le\varepsilon$;
the reward-gain ceiling $\range(r)^2/\beta$ of Theorem~\ref{thm:driftcap} reports what has been
given up.

\section{Discussion}

\subsection{What the results say about reward hacking}

Reward hacking is usually described as the phenomenon that optimizing a proxy $r$ degrades an
unmeasured target $f$. The results above decompose it into a \emph{budget} factor
$\Theta(\sigma_p(r)/\beta)$, bounding how much total probability mass alignment can relocate,
and a \emph{direction} factor $\Cov_p(r,f)$, determining how much of that relocation is visible
in the coordinate $f$. Both are computed under the reference policy, so the leading-order damage
to any audit statistic is predicted by a quantity measurable before alignment begins.

The results also delimit what the anchor cannot do. It cannot protect a statistic strongly
correlated with the reward --- for such $f$ the first-order gap $\Cov_p(r,f)/\beta$ is genuinely
present; Theorem~\ref{thm:varsharp} exhibits a family where the bound is attained up to a factor
$6$. And it cannot protect anything at small $\beta$, where Theorem~\ref{thm:collapse} shows
collapse is total.

\subsection{Range versus variance, and rare exploits}

A widespread intuition holds that a reward model with a large exploitable maximum is dangerous
in proportion to that maximum. Theorem~\ref{thm:vardrift} says otherwise at fixed high $\beta$:
what governs drift is the reward's fluctuation weighted by the reference policy. A hole the
reference essentially never falls into contributes $\delta(1-\delta)R^2$, not $R^2$. Danger from
such holes is therefore not a first-order tilting effect but a consequence of leaving the
perturbative regime --- exactly the regime shown discontinuous in Theorem~\ref{thm:collapse}.

\subsection{Methodological remark: which step is loose}

The progression Theorem~\ref{thm:selflimit} $\to$ Theorem~\ref{thm:quad} $\to$
Theorem~\ref{thm:vardrift} is a case study in locating slack. The first law was obtained by
composing a divergence bound with Pinsker; Theorem~\ref{thm:pinskersharp} proves the Pinsker step
optimal, so all slack lay in the divergence bound, and two successive improvements of that step
produced the correct law. Verify the sharpness of each step of a composite bound separately
before attempting to improve the composition.

\subsection{Scope and limitations}

The development is for a finite response space and a positive reference policy; finiteness is
used only for existence of $\max$, $\min$ and finite sums, and every statement extends to a
reference measure with bounded reward under standard integrability assumptions. All statements
concern the exact optimizer $\pi_\beta$ (or, in Theorem~\ref{thm:ptx}, any policy beating the
reference); they do not account for optimization error, clipped surrogate objectives, or the
reward model's own estimation error. They are the ideal-case geometry against which those error
sources must be measured.

\section{Future directions}

\paragraph{The absolute constant in the drift law.}
We have $\|\pi_\beta-p\|_1=\Theta(\sigma_p(r)/\beta)$ with proven bracket
$[\sigma/(2\beta),3\sigma/\beta]$ on an explicit family. The cumulant heuristic predicts a leading
constant of $1$; establishing $\|\pi_\beta-p\|_1\le(1+o(1))\sigma_p(r)/\beta$ requires replacing
the $e^{\range(r)/\beta}$ prefactor --- which enters only through the Lipschitz estimate on the
tilt --- by a second-moment argument.

\paragraph{A sharp threshold for audit statistics.}
Theorem~\ref{thm:firstorder} gives a first-order law but no phase transition. Is there a critical
temperature $\beta_c$, expressible through $\sigma_p(r)$, $\Cov_p(r,f)$ and higher cumulants,
below which the audit gap ceases to be predicted by the covariance? The zero-temperature behaviour
shows such a threshold must exist.

\paragraph{Higher-order expansion.}
The natural continuation is an Edgeworth-type series whose second-order term involves the third
joint cumulant of $(r,f)$ under $p$, with remainder $O(\beta^{-3})$; this would sharpen
Algorithm B from a ranking to a calibrated prediction.

\paragraph{Multi-objective and iterated alignment.}
Composition of tilts is a tilt by the sum of rewards, so a single round with
$r=\sum_i\lambda_i r_i$ is covered verbatim; the iterated setting, where each round re-estimates
the reward on the previous round's policy, is not a single tilt and its drift accumulation is
unstudied.

\paragraph{Beyond the exact optimizer.}
Quantifying degradation for an $\epsilon$-suboptimal policy is immediate for
Theorem~\ref{thm:ptx} but open for the covariance expansion, which uses the exact exponential
form of $\pi_\beta$.

\section{Conclusion}

The Kullback--Leibler anchor in preference optimization admits a complete and sharp quantitative
theory on finite response spaces. The aligned policy is the exponential tilt of the reference;
its displacement is $\Theta(\sigma_p(r)/\beta)$, governed by the reward's fluctuation under the
reference rather than by its range; every observable's shift is, to first order, its reference
covariance with the reward divided by $\beta$, with an explicit $O(\beta^{-2})$ remainder; the
reward gain is squeezed between $\beta\KL$ from below and $\tfrac12\range(r)\|\pi_\beta-p\|_1$
from above; the pretraining mix-in contributes an additive, symmetric budget; and all of this
degenerates into total collapse at zero temperature. The folklore that the divergence penalty
prevents reward hacking is true, but the useful form of the statement is finer: it prevents
first-order movement of exactly those behaviours uncorrelated with the reward model, and the
correlation can be measured before any alignment is performed.

